r-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13666e1e-c022-5b9a-b071-b81546a1b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-80845" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in Z∣n+1\\ge -1}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n-2\\ge -4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $B⊈A$\n- **IV.** $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388c46a0-2156-5b94-b255-eb7d8c824a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451815} {:answer/id #uuid "cf09000e-81b6-5c6f-8edd-ce69a7693399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "388c46a0-2156-5b94