#edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5521ee3b-fffd-5558-98b1-b81f3a08c9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81164" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(1,1)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f991bde2-a2dd-5997-85e7-48c0a588560b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451833} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f6253-6648-51da-9f05-9207335fa7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f6253-6648-51da-9f05-9207335fa7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451834} :answer-field/id #uuid "11d045a4-5991-5337-ad9c-b832845e2dd9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452764} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e65ce071-7dc1-5100-b660-e89d39f58038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451835} {:answer/id #uuid "74ba8c4c-687e-5876-b882-dc0a2a63b75f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e65ce071-7dc1-5100-b660-e89d39f58038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451835} :answer-field/id #uuid "21fd0057-7bf7-54ce-93d9-6dc035cb7868" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452765} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1d878b3-13f7-5a7a-8b4e-92252de9b06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451837} {:answer/id #uuid "be8adabd-e2a9-54e8-9cf4-7b11d060aab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1d878b3-13f7-5a7a-8b4e-92252de9b06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451837} :answer-field/id #uuid "4669d2fb-acce-57bc-8e15-cf8d7a5bfb3b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43f0f1fc-04b9-5788-b436-d0d8e07500dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81375" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}+{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\n- Statement III is false. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nSo the integral converges.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87131d2a-00fa-5c86-adb5-5dcd9781663d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451839} {:answer/id #uuid "c5992e5f-0eac-5510-8b9e-4c9bdb590e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5992e5f-0eac-5510-8b9e-4c9bdb590e1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451840} :answer-field/id #uuid "f556a776-7b3e-5b87-96a3-38f6c6a85224" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94e1abfb-4ac1-5387-8365-44f8be67855e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451841} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae75960-5f9e-5be6-9aa4-ca852b78c6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e1abfb-4ac1-5387-8365-44f8be67855e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451841} :answer-field/id #uuid "4203a946-5791-54e5-8948-df9e4bceec16" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452768} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3316e667-7cb8-5d37-8ca1-05b94216632d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451843} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ec2c90-b24e-58ba-a3ab-9443e572892a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3316e667-7cb8-5d37-8ca1-05b94216632d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451843} :answer-field/id #uuid "038959f0-30da-57d8-900b-1c0a7eef35de" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e8066ca0-5e8f-5876-924e-8be55bf1f74e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82290" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n- **II.** $A$ has two identical rows\n- **III.** $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=4\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor a zero row.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb81d4ca-b624-5bd6-b5d6-8848a582c1a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451851} {:answer/id #uuid "072d8636-45df-522c-a21c-11c80be3c9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072d8636-45df-522c-a21c-11c80be3c9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451852} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ead3048-fa8d-5561-beeb-c20e86a46f07" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452773} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f8ac0c-f828-5cbe-b1a1-9ac2fe52a581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451853} {:answer/id #uuid "78608524-9f24-5352-8c0a-c522e7bb20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19f8ac0c-f828-5cbe-b1a1-9ac2fe52a581" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451853} :answer-field/id #uuid "416ea6c4-aa3e-5622-ad1a-5f5c04bbc3f8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa1ef54f-6746-5e13-8812-1a43c4af52f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451855} {:answer/id #uuid "258461fb-779d-533f-b3c8-2a096b2dcdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "258461fb-779d-533f-b3c8-2a096b2dcdc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451856} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bed2a41-9fc5-5095-9e22-bb769fd4e778" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6582765-66f3-5651-9e94-833ca793221c" :question/math-academy-id "q-84347" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $P?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- **II.** ${P}^{-1}$ is **not** orthogonal\n- **III.** $-P$ is orthogonal" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the product ${P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1}\\cdot ({P}^{-1})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-P)\\cdot (-P)^{T} & =(-P)\\cdot (-{P}^{T}) \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041bfe5b-590c-52a1-8abd-392d6a06b4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451857} {:answer/id #uuid "bd73da35-a2bd-569b-aa95-e7ace5216014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "041bfe5b-590c-52a1-8abd-392d6a06b4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451857} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9fb4fbc-f699-56e7-8e73-ae2329de9b16" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452776} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6208240c-6e58-5465-95d8-6fd29d0111db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451859} {:answer/id #uuid "4e21d8f3-1ca2-507a-bffd-6e6526106a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e21d8f3-1ca2-507a-bffd-6e6526106a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451860} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f64777b-1c75-5375-87b9-c74f54bdadeb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452777} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06b30fc-d6a0-5b6e-a013-87d9ef07f287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451861} {:answer/id #uuid "01225e45-0e4a-533b-a1f7-48bca794d141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a06b30fc-d6a0-5b6e-a013-87d9ef07f287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451861} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d0e01e-ccc5-5919-850a-7e7c5a404273" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "55240a89-c0cc-59a9-b892-0f2b31965d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-84352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5Q$ is also orthogonal\n- **II.** If $A=QB{Q}^{T}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n- **III.** ${Q}^{3}$ is the zero matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5Q\\cdot (5Q)^{T} & =5Q\\cdot 5{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =25I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(QB{Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(Q)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({Q}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's consider the identity matrix\n\n$$\nQ=I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that $Q$ is an orthogonal matrix and also ${Q}^{3}=Q\\ne O.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "100ab73e-3dad-5b15-9da2-d244c67bcc9b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Double Integrals Over Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14078cbb-e2f9-5c26-80b6-ce16aa62ca33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451869} {:answer/id #uuid "618b19c8-3470-50fe-b37b-4cc4086849aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14078cbb-e2f9-5c26-80b6-ce16aa62ca33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451869} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd4d02ca-f845-54bf-97b7-803e44eb3ff2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62be857-de11-55f2-bf65-a6f6fa2b4ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451871} {:answer/id #uuid "63d3abf6-f57f-54e2-a190-75a48dc84fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d62be857-de11-55f2-bf65-a6f6fa2b4ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451871} :answer-field/id #uuid "eebb9ca8-6ae2-54a4-8b8d-d24fe6daf795" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452783} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d54569e8-25cd-51fb-9e4c-cbeb92e1913c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451873} {:answer/id #uuid "4a739055-caf5-5dad-a093-92704b147e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a739055-caf5-5dad-a093-92704b147e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451874} :answer-field/id #uuid "edc906f1-6059-5fa6-9455-d997269a94cd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "020f4792-4587-5aa1-be12-a9175f71b2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86867" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\n- **II.** ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\n- **III.** ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/62/62925844de27c9731b181f17a1bdd912bfa3265176bd62ebda425d182ae8d9e8.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not completely cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c94cffac-0f52-5c77-825f-0bfdd28683ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451875} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6eac80-7bf7-51e7-804d-525613d820b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e6eac80-7bf7-51e7-804d-525613d820b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb2c1dd-50b0-53e4-bf0b-e1ff47d01cee" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e3a6b98-e86c-5623-b6e5-022fb623e2fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451877} {:answer/id #uuid "032c98a4-c431-548b-8431-6d9ded716e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451878}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032c98a4-c431-548b-8431-6d9ded716e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451878} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58dbdbb-b79d-54a5-b153-4dab46952d6a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452786} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b17e375e-f3a5-5985-8f28-d894632d69a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451879} {:answer/id #uuid "860ed8f0-ec8b-5919-8e7d-49b72f59cfda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b17e375e-f3a5-5985-8f28-d894632d69a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451879} :answer-field/id #uuid "875dbb3d-f76a-54a9-b745-3dcb5a12f6f8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72dda5b9-2988-5be6-b882-d73d509880d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-89885" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x+y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(-1,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(-1,1)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(-1,1)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(-1,1),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =1+(x+y) \\\\  & =x+y+1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)} & ={(x+y+1)|}_{(-1,1)} \\\\  & =-1+1+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(-1,1)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(-1,1).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(-1,1)}>0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d15a71-3554-597f-a996-a97ed1c8ed55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451881} {:answer/id #uuid "99eada67-2d94-5b32-9e19-3d6bc263054a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99eada67-2d94-5b32-9e19-3d6bc263054a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451882} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ce40fc1-4bfd-5742-8051-a80ebafcd454" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38078b3e-4d70-583f-a52d-847f3095a4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451883} {:answer/id #uuid "f69868c8-5873-5d0e-9fec-51b390471e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38078b3e-4d70-583f-a52d-847f3095a4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451883} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3d33c25-6a45-59ec-8f30-8a183e2e2482" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452789} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868ad87a-a205-508f-99d3-24bf6b4649dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451885} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2cc099-7c1a-5887-b247-8493f04bd55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868ad87a-a205-508f-99d3-24bf6b4649dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451885} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ffba87-d42c-5fd0-9996-eccc8e91df84" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21b379b1-3944-5c72-96df-e324e7834ad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89886" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=y-x.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(0,0)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(0,0)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(0,0),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-1 \\\\  & =(y-x)-1 \\\\  & =y-x-1 \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)} & ={(y-x-1)|}_{(0,0)} \\\\  & =0-0-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There is a critical point at $(0,0).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(0,0)}<0,\n$$\n\nwe have a local maximum (not a local minimum) at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f901a3ab-3b58-51a1-b653-f88ac4b09060" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Derivative Test To Classify a Point on a Solution Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdbb3904-c6b6-532f-af12-bb5ee642b06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451887} {:answer/id #uuid "ec88bb3b-8bc6-53e6-b80b-c5c7e170ee59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec88bb3b-8bc6-53e6-b80b-c5c7e170ee59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451888} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed9d8978-02ae-54de-b476-495ad65f4d05" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdf6f3d-eff4-5e01-af8b-22be8618f8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451889} {:answer/id #uuid "99928429-3b36-5da2-927f-e66de30e2901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99928429-3b36-5da2-927f-e66de30e2901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451890} :answer-field/id #uuid "355746cc-2404-539d-a78a-26147b7e8e68" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452792} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a69ba8d-ce87-5da6-8868-94df457dd58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451891} {:answer/id #uuid "5177442b-57e5-5591-b061-29c7ac96dfdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a69ba8d-ce87-5da6-8868-94df457dd58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451891} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ea8114-05b9-5333-b6e1-b560b2d1e70b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d8694a2-ea00-53e6-abdb-fa32dda7bdcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-89889" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ satisfies the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nGiven that the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f$\n- **II.** $(1,4)$ is a local minimum point of the function $f$\n- **III.** $(1,4)$ is an inflection point of the function $f$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the first and second derivatives of the function at the given point.\n\nTo find the first derivative of $f$ at $(1,4),$ we substitute the coordinates of our point into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)} & ={({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =(1)^{3}+3(1)^{2}-(4) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the second derivative, we first need to differentiate the equation and then substitute the coordinates of the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+6x+y \\\\ {\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)} & ={(-{x}^{3}+6x+y)|}_{(1,4)} \\\\  & =-(1)^{3}+6(1)+(4) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,4)}=0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that $(1,4)$ is a stationary point of the function $f.$\n\n- Statement II is true. There is a critical point at $(1,4).$ So, according to the second derivative test, since \n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{(1,4)}>0,\n$$\n\n \nwe have a local minimum at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the second derivative is not zero, there is no inflection point at $(1,4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6302f81d-4834-56a5-b014-0a97c3dccd6f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About General Solutions of Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb85e8eb-30f1-5e30-88c6-2582220a10a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451893} {:answer/id #uuid "951c19d3-6d68-50a9-9a17-2c166c688f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "951c19d3-6d68-50a9-9a17-2c166c688f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451894} :answer-field/id #uuid "db880168-c319-51c9-8b78-70142c95d5ec" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452794} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9432fd1-b127-528c-9c36-1845ecfb49e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451895} {:answer/id #uuid "a669c147-cbc3-57f4-b038-ac51d2ff09fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451896}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a669c147-cbc3-57f4-b038-ac51d2ff09fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451896} :answer-field/id #uuid "050c8270-4795-5516-8572-0dd11186bc74" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9611986-1e99-5e63-bd2e-d8b8e11bc95d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451897} {:answer/id #uuid "5642b8fb-b64d-532e-a7f5-75734f821b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451898}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5642b8fb-b64d-532e-a7f5-75734f821b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451898} :answer-field/id #uuid "900c0ac8-9b40-5d86-815d-5a4462740f32" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd483d10-bcb0-5d3a-aec6-2a397c70d709" :question/math-academy-id "q-90699" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+16y=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **II.** ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$ is a solution to the equation.\n- **III.** The general solution of the equation is $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement I is true.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\n-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+16(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)=0.\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian.\n\nThe Wronskian $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ of two functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nW({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)}^{′}-{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)-(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡4x+{sin}^{2}⁡4x) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not equal to $0,$ the functions are linearly independent, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x$  is the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e20bb352-8798-5219-b654-623357df644c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451899} {:answer/id #uuid "a6004465-ae7d-59c8-806f-2866bb916848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20bb352-8798-5219-b654-623357df644c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451899} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc542675-dd46-5683-afc4-6c9bb8629ebe" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "372b0b95-a59f-563d-9630-745c738700e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451901} {:answer/id #uuid "d17fb8d0-e7d2-5ca7-b6c3-b47f98ebca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "372b0b95-a59f-563d-9630-745c738700e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b928d863-ca43-5ecd-b2ec-156c93fbb974" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452798} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa9d8b21-4783-5ada-a5a1-249a2982a947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451903} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4582d3-b5d4-568a-8c7f-88ee00ab3f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e4582d3-b5d4-568a-8c7f-88ee00ab3f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451904} :answer-field/id #uuid "b378b982-cafe-5b13-ac67-c1e2b582eb1a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21aff269-b5d0-5ff1-9c3e-386be14a7e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-94287" :question/problem "A particle of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=24\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{4}y=0$\n- **II.** The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- **III.** The general solution can be expressed as $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{24}{6}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{2}{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =2$ in form 1 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is true.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a459ec25-988e-5f91-8c25-2b7b81b6b6ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of a Mass-Spring System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f29c74d-4e14-5a24-9683-ca67874f9d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451905} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa81a14-0980-5d4c-8e00-12da53182396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fa81a14-0980-5d4c-8e00-12da53182396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451906} :answer-field/id #uuid "c84cb828-9a1a-5639-b4d9-d43a955bd3fe" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de19cc85-076b-5377-9445-ebde1b72e493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451907} {:answer/id #uuid "977a2f5b-333e-55c8-8da3-ad1a628caac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de19cc85-076b-5377-9445-ebde1b72e493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451907} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea20260-a271-5a10-a832-9ccf49b5a917" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452801} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd46bc35-f774-5fe1-bea5-72f5ef16cda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451909} {:answer/id #uuid "474438b9-96d0-5310-a09f-25adb3dfb41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd46bc35-f774-5fe1-bea5-72f5ef16cda9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451909} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dc124a8-736f-53ee-8bc6-a7ac01651b4e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de4a4e60-2947-5dae-80ca-6bd02414f29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94288" :question/problem "A particle of mass $7\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=21\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$ The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. \n\nIf the particle moves with simple harmonic motion, then which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The governing differential equation is ${y}^{″}+3y=0$\n- **II.** The frequency $f$ of the oscillation is $f=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}$\n- **III.** The general solution can be expressed as $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ),$ where $R$ is the amplitude and $ϕ$ is the phase." :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with simple harmonic motion (SHM) under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{k}{m}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing this to the SHM equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe see that\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{k}{m}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $k$ and $m$ gives\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{21}{7}=3\\,⟹\\,\\omega =\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the governing differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The frequency $f$ (measured in ${s}^{-1}$) is given by\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{1}{T}=\\frac{\\omega }{2\\pi },\n$$\n\nwhere $T$ is the period. So in our case, we have\n\n$$\nf=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,.\n$$\n\nSo the frequency $f=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2\\pi }\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- The general solution for $y(t)$ can take one of three forms.\n\n- $A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{1})$\n\n- $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+{ϕ}_{2})$\n\nWriting $\\omega =\\sqrt{3}$ and ${ϕ}_{1}=ϕ$ in form 2 gives\n\n$$\ny(t)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{3}t+ϕ).\n$$\n\nHence, statement III is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ac6cdc-2746-5b43-bd9a-71f9e2b88f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451911} {:answer/id #uuid "a6a60923-dd24-5397-8514-f00555707a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a60923-dd24-5397-8514-f00555707a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451912} :answer-field/id #uuid "882b1b0e-7667-58fa-a610-b29f39cea8f0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452803} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dc9b38-8d8b-526d-8109-de5a6641a5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451913} {:answer/id #uuid "767cb5ef-5c58-5fa0-86e4-07da2cc8995b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dc9b38-8d8b-526d-8109-de5a6641a5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451913} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f567160-672e-57ca-8d2a-ce2f2ac2cef7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452804} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411455db-fa35-5440-b800-17f849ed6393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451915} {:answer/id #uuid "90a5c468-08a8-52b4-a291-d994b5c9ea87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "411455db-fa35-5440-b800-17f849ed6393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451915} :answer-field/id #uuid "63875c8e-e107-53d2-b46a-269a684f3997" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8e4b9d3-5113-5f39-9a2d-eee9d1949dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94310" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be03-fb7e54ecbd7c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Types of Damped Oscillations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b173ff0-9b1a-5c3a-9553-9e2122b40871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451917} {:answer/id #uuid "0da075d0-9182-5b2b-a41c-1a2c5f285c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da075d0-9182-5b2b-a41c-1a2c5f285c26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451918} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c53b5b-9481-53c7-8ced-5646f548a0a3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e660c83-18f6-55ad-8e5a-5e4aecb8546a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451919} {:answer/id #uuid "0cd25faa-e362-5233-be6b-98f26578eed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cd25faa-e362-5233-be6b-98f26578eed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451920} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecd5099b-4d00-56e1-9017-fb47c78b0397" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f62fad2-48d0-5e14-b686-4024f93cdd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451921} {:answer/id #uuid "5df8d53d-dfe7-5e8b-88a3-63034b3e0dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5df8d53d-dfe7-5e8b-88a3-63034b3e0dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451922} :answer-field/id #uuid "af024238-6d48-5b5d-9c32-077d25b27679" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6276abb-11ab-5f2c-8db5-0bce52daa784" :question/math-academy-id "q-94311" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping\n\n- **II.** ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping\n\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" :question/worked-solution "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =28>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+7y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +7=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(7) & =-3<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement II is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={8}^{2}-4(1)(16) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "089c1139-6e3a-5240-be0